<div class="book-title">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim</div>
<div class="halacha-title">Hilchos Netilas Yadayim L'Seudah — Betzi'as HaPas<br><span style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:400;">(Part 4: §§ 44–56)</span></div>
<div class="lm-subtitle">Based on <span class="v">"Ki Merachamam Yenahaigem"</span> <span class="sr">(<span class="lr">LM II, 7</span>)</span> — continuation.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 44 — Silence in the End of Days</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Therefore the wise man at that time shall be silent"</span> <span class="sr">(Amos 5:13)</span>. <span class="v">"To You, silence is praise, O G-d, in Tzion"</span> <span class="sr">(Psalms 65:2)</span>. <span class="v">"Only to G-d be my soul silent"</span> <span class="sr">(Psalms 62:6)</span>. <span class="v">"It is good to wait in silence for the salvation of Hashem"</span> <span class="sr">(Lamentations 3:26)</span>. And many such verses. For now, in these <span class="kc">end of days</span> about which these verses were prophesied, one must <span class="kc">be silent</span> greatly about everything happening in the world — not to enter into investigations, <span class="h">kushyos</span>, and <span class="h">terutzim</span> at all — only to be silent and wait for the salvation of Hashem. Until, for the most part, through the silence, his heart will burn until he cries out and prays to Hashem, in the aspect of: <span class="v">"I was mute in silence, I was still… my heart grew hot within me; in my musing a fire burned; I spoke with my tongue"</span> <span class="sr">(Psalms 39:3-4)</span>.</div>

<div class="p">And this is: <span class="v">"He enlarges nations and destroys them"</span> <span class="sr">(Job 12:23)</span> — <span class="v">"He captures the wise in their own craftiness"</span> <span class="sr">(Job 5:13)</span>. All of this was at <span class="h">Krias Yam Suf</span>, where [Hashem] deliberately surrounded them to mislead [the Egyptians], as it is written: <span class="v">"Let them turn back and encamp before Pi HaChiros…"</span> <span class="v">"And Pharaoh said…"</span> And so it is now.</div>

<div class="p">And those who are <span class="kc">pursued</span> must be careful not to walk in cleverness — only to cry out to Hashem. And if even that is difficult, at the very least not to question [His ways], and certainly not to speak against His attributes — only to be silent and wait, <span class="v">"until He looks down and sees"</span> <span class="sr">(Lamentations 3:50)</span>, and to await His kindness alone: <span class="v">"Hashem shall fight for you, and you shall be silent"</span>. As it is written: <span class="v">"We do not know what to do, but our eyes are upon You"</span> <span class="sr">(II Chronicles 20:12)</span>, and: <span class="v">"For we have no strength against this great multitude… help us, for we lean upon You"</span> <span class="sr">(II Chronicles 14:10)</span>.</div>

<div class="p">Especially now, at the end of this exile, about which Yirmiyahu said: <span class="v">"Waters flowed over my head… I called Your Name, Hashem, from the lowest pit"</span> <span class="sr">(Lamentations 3:54-55)</span>, and: <span class="v">"It is good to wait in silence for the salvation of Hashem"</span> — <span class="v">"until He looks down and sees from heaven, for Hashem will not cast off forever"</span>.</div>

<div class="p">For the essential persecution of the pursuers — whether from the nations or among Israel itself, where the disputants fiercely pursue the upright — is mainly through <span class="kc">empty cleverness</span>: everyone imagining himself wise and understanding, wanting everyone to follow his crooked path. And it seems to him that he must pursue the pursued. But the pursued must be especially careful not to be misled by their evil smooth words — only to look at the truth for truth's sake, and to fulfill: <span class="v">"Do not answer a fool according to his folly"</span> <span class="sr">(Proverbs 26:4)</span>. Only to be silent, even in one's mind — not to enter into <span class="h">kushyos</span> and <span class="h">terutzim</span> — only to await the salvation of Hashem, in the aspect of what Moshe told Israel at <span class="h">Krias Yam Suf</span>: <span class="v">"Fear not, stand firm and see the salvation of Hashem… Hashem shall fight for you, and you shall be silent."</span></div>

<div class="p">Only now, since a complete redemption is needed with no exile afterward, and the <span class="h">sitra achra</span> and <span class="h">yetzer hara</span> are spreading enormously, one must extend his patience even more and strengthen himself to be silent in his mind — not to question Hashem or the true <span class="h">yir'ei Hashem</span>, but to strive to cry out and pray to Hashem greatly. As the <span class="h">Tanna</span> repeated many times: <span class="mx">"In the footsteps of <span class="h">Mashiach</span>, insolence will increase… those who fear sin [will be despised]… truth will be absent"</span> — and our Sages expounded that truth will be made into "flocks upon flocks," each one saying the truth is with him — until the conclusion each time: <span class="mx">"Upon whom can we lean? Only upon our Father in heaven."</span> This is repeated multiple times, and each time the confusion and accusation of these end of days is revealed, it concludes: upon whom can we lean if not upon our Father in heaven? — as explained in <span class="h">Sotah</span>, see there.</div>

<div class="p">The essence: the main pursuit of the pursuers against the upright and the sincere is that they want to ensnare them with their evil cleverness and abundant mockery — and this is the hardest of all, for the main thing is the <span class="kc">prevention of the mind</span>. First they pursue them openly; then they try even harder to seduce them with smooth words, claiming they seek their good — and this is hardest of all. Therefore it is impossible to describe everything involved — only: <span class="kc">the wise man at that time shall be silent</span> and understand where the truth is, and fulfill the words of the <span class="h">Tannaim</span> who repeated: upon whom can we lean if not upon our Father in heaven.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 45 — Purification of the Blood Through Eating</div>

<div class="p">Let us return to the matter of the counting. It is explained in the <span class="h">Kavanos</span> that during the <span class="h">Sefirah</span> we engage in rectifying and purifying the <span class="kc">blood</span>, the main thing being that the blood should be <span class="kc">absorbed into the limbs</span> — awesome secrets regarding this. For this is the overall matter of Pesach and the counting, the aspect of <span class="v">"In your blood, live!"</span> <span class="sr">(Ezekiel 16:6)</span>. And it is known that all the secrets of <span class="h">Kabbalah</span> are hinted at within man, who is the aspect of the <span class="h">tzelem Elokim</span>.</div>

<div class="p">According to the Torah teaching <span class="v">"Ki Merachamam"</span>: Moshe Rabbainu informed us of His G-dliness, that <span class="v">"the whole earth is full of His glory"</span> — we saw it with our own eyes through the signs and wonders and through the illumination of <span class="h">da'as</span> he illuminated in us. Through the <span class="h">da'as</span> — whose main thing is to include the upper and lower worlds together — one merits the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> during eating. Therefore we engage in the <span class="h">tikkun</span> of eating during the counting and <span class="h">Shavuos</span>, through the <span class="h">Omer</span> of barley and the two loaves. Therefore during the days of the counting the <span class="h">man</span> [manna] descended for Israel. Therefore we then request: <span class="v">"The earth has given its produce; may [G-d] bless us"</span> <span class="sr">(Psalms 67:7)</span> — for then the field crops grow, and we engage in the <span class="h">tikkunim</span> so that we merit the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> during eating, the essential inclusiveness of the worlds.</div>

<div class="p">This is the <span class="kc">purification of the blood</span>: the main thing is that the blood be absorbed into the limbs. All this is accomplished through the <span class="h">tikkun</span> of eating — when one strives to draw upon himself during eating the <span class="h">yirah</span> that approaches a person then, until he draws the wondrous illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span>.</div>

<div class="p">For it is known: the main point of eating — sustaining a person — is for the sake of the blood, for <span class="v">"the blood is the soul"</span> <span class="sr">(Deuteronomy 12:23)</span>, and it gives life to the person. Due to the boiling of the blood, there is a burning fire in a person — and therefore one needs to eat, introducing food into the digestive system, which serves as a furnace. Through this, the blood is vitalized and can sustain the person. But the main thing is to <span class="kc">eat in holiness</span> according to the Torah, in the aspect of <span class="v">"Your Torah is within my innards"</span> <span class="sr">(Psalms 40:9)</span> — meaning: through the food, one vitalizes the blood in which the soul resides, until the soul is bonded with the body in the aspect of inclusiveness of the worlds — upper and lower, soul and body.</div>

<div class="p">Until one merits complete <span class="h">yirah</span> — the aspect of holy <span class="h">gevuros</span>, the aspect of blood — arriving at the wondrous illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> — the holy heat of the blood, burning and blazing for Hashem in flames of love: <span class="v">"Its sparks are sparks of fire"</span> <span class="sr">(Song of Songs 8:6)</span>, <span class="v">"My heart grew hot within me"</span>. This is the essential absorption of blood into the limbs — the aspect of <span class="h">Adam</span>: the <span class="h">Aleph</span> illuminates and vitalizes the <span class="h">dam</span> [blood] of <span class="h">Adam</span>, and it becomes <span class="h">mah</span> [45] — the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span>.</div>

<div class="p">This is accomplished only through Israel's eating — they are called <span class="h">tzadikim</span> — eating in the manner described above, meriting <span class="h">yirah</span> and <span class="h">Ratzon</span>, from which the essential satisfaction [derives]: <span class="v">"and satisfies every living being with <span class="h">Ratzon</span>"</span> — <span class="v">"the righteous eats to satisfy his soul."</span> As the Holy Zohar says: <span class="mx">"the <span class="h">Ratzon</span> of satisfaction rests upon him."</span> But when one does not eat in holiness, it is the aspect of <span class="v">"the belly of the wicked shall lack."</span> For the essential satisfaction is from <span class="h">Ratzon</span>. And then the blood that receives vitality from the food is impure — there is <span class="kc">turbidity of the blood</span> — the blood does not bond and absorb properly into the limbs but rather rushes and flows over them. From this comes all confusion of <span class="h">da'as</span>, derived from turbid blood — from this, all <span class="h">ruach shtus</span>.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore the essential <span class="h">tikkun</span>: draw close to the <span class="h">tzadik</span>, the aspect of Moshe, who has the power to illuminate <span class="h">da'as</span> — informing him that Hashem is still with him — until he strives to be a man of valor, drawing upon himself the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> during eating. Through this comes essential satisfaction — the life of the blood — the blood being properly absorbed into the limbs, in the aspect of <span class="v">"in your blood, live!"</span> Through this, one transforms the evil desires of the boiling blood, turning the heat of the blood to holiness — accustoming himself to direct his <span class="h">Ratzon</span> always to Hashem. For <span class="mx">"when this one rises, that one falls"</span> — two thoughts cannot coexist. When one accustoms himself to a strong <span class="h">Ratzon</span> for Hashem constantly — especially during eating, when <span class="h">Ratzon</span> shines most — then one can draw it upon himself most, if he is not a lazy man (<span class="h">shlimzalnik</span>, as explained there). Through this, the evil desires of boiling blood on the side of impurity are nullified — the blood that was not properly absorbed, like the child brought to Rebbi Noson the Babylonian, who was exceedingly red (as brought in the <span class="h">Kavanos</span>).</div>

<div class="p">Through this — the <span class="h">Ratzon</span> drawn during eating that bonds soul with body — this is the purification of the blood, absorbed into the limbs properly, burning for Hashem at all times with wondrous <span class="h">Ratzon</span>. Until one expresses the <span class="h">Ratzon</span> with his mouth — thanking, praising, and praying to Hashem. This is the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> of <span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span> after eating. In the aspect of: <span class="v">"my heart grew hot within me; in my musing a fire burned; I spoke with my tongue."</span> For the essential heat of the heart burning like fire comes from the boiling of the blood — one must turn it to holiness, burning for Hashem with holy desires, speaking with his tongue his yearnings and good desires. The essential revelation of holy <span class="h">emunah</span> is through speech: <span class="v">"I will make Your faithfulness known with my mouth"</span> — and through this, the <span class="h">makifin</span> are drawn through speech.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 46 — The Praise of <i>Ratzon</i>: The <i>Nessi'im</i> and the Wagons</div>

<div class="p">Now come and see what I found in the Midrash <span class="h">Chazis</span> of <span class="h">Shir HaShirim</span> regarding the greatness of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> merited through the hands — the <span class="h">remazim</span> in <span class="h">yam hachochmah</span>. The Midrash on <span class="h">Shir HaShirim</span> (Chapter 6, verse 4): <span class="v">"You are beautiful, My beloved, as <span class="h">Tirtzah</span>"</span> — <span class="kc">when you want [<span class="h">rotzah</span>], when you desire, you do not need to request anything, to learn from anyone.</span> Who told [the <span class="h">nessi'im</span>] to bring wagons and oxen to carry the <span class="h">Mishkan</span>? Was it not from themselves, of their own accord?</div>

<div class="p">Pay close attention to every word of this holy Midrash — how greatly it praises the holy <span class="h">Ratzon</span> of Israel, the holy nation: when they truly want with holy desires to draw close to Hashem, <span class="kc">they do not need to ask anyone or learn from anyone</span>. The <span class="h">Ratzon</span> itself informs them what to do to give pleasure to Hashem. As the Midrash says: <span class="v">"When you want — you do not need to request anything, to learn from anyone."</span></div>

<div class="p">For <span class="h">Ratzon</span> is the aspect of the light of <span class="h">mah</span> that shines in <span class="h">yam hachochmah</span> — the <span class="h">remazim</span>. One understands on his own through the <span class="h">remazim</span> that the <span class="h">tzadik</span> hints to him at all times through his holy Torah teachings — for it is impossible to explain everything by mouth and in writing — only through <span class="h">remazim</span> can he illuminate in one's <span class="h">da'as</span> until one strengthens with very strong desires for Hashem, until one does not even know what one desires (as explained there) — until Hashem helps him and illuminates his heart to understand what to do, to draw close to Him. Like the <span class="h">nessi'im</span>, who understood on their own to bring wagons for the <span class="h">Mishkan</span> even though they were not commanded at all.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 47 — The <i>Mishkan</i>, the Degalim, and the Colors of Israel</div>

<div class="p">All of this was at the erection of the <span class="h">Mishkan</span>, when the <span class="h">nessi'im</span> brought wagons. The essential <span class="h">tikkunim</span> of the Torah teaching are accomplished through the <span class="h">Mishkan</span> — the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> — where all sacrifices were offered <span class="v">"for a pleasing [<span class="h">l'ratzon</span>] fragrance."</span> The essential inclusiveness of upper and lower was through the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"You are beautiful, My beloved, as <span class="h">Tirtzah</span>, comely as Yerushalayim, awesome as bannered hosts"</span> <span class="sr">(Song of Songs 6:4)</span>. <span class="v">"Awesome as bannered hosts"</span> speaks of the greatness of Israel's <span class="kc"><span class="h">degalim</span></span> [banners/flags] — Israel traveled in the wilderness in the order of the banners, carrying the <span class="h">Mishkan</span> among them. All of this is the aspect of all the <span class="h">tikkunim</span> of the Torah teaching: the main thing is to illuminate the holy <span class="h">da'as</span> in Israel — informing them always that <span class="v">"Hashem — He is the G-d"</span> — so they never forget Him. As it is written: <span class="v">"Fortunate is the man who does not forget You"</span>. Through this, Israel is extracted from sins, from <span class="h">ruach shtus</span>. This must be received from the true <span class="h">tzadik</span>, the aspect of Moshe. And also: <span class="kc">every member of Israel must illuminate in his fellow</span> the illumination of <span class="h">da'as</span> that was drawn into his heart from his <span class="h">Rebbe</span> — all Israel illuminating one another, <span class="mx">"receiving from one another."</span></div>

<div class="p">This is the greatness of the <span class="h">degalim</span>: all Israel traveled around the <span class="h">Mishkan</span> (where the <span class="h">da'as</span> of Moshe primarily shone, for there He communicated many <span class="h">mitzvos</span> of the Torah from the <span class="h">Ohel Mo'ed</span>). They carried the banners upon which were depicted the <span class="kc">colors of the twelve stones of the <span class="h">Choshen</span></span> — the supernal colors, holy colors, present in every member of Israel, in the aspect of <span class="v">"Israel, in whom I am glorified"</span> <span class="sr">(Isaiah 49:3)</span> — comprised of many colors. All these colors of Israel were included in the holy depictions of the banners, carried as signs — the splendor and beauty and awesome glory of the holy camps of Israel.</div>

<div class="p">All this so that every holy color of every member of Israel would <span class="kc">illuminate in his fellow</span>, and his fellow in his fellow — <span class="mx">"receiving from one another"</span> — so that upon each person the holy <span class="h">da'as</span> of Moshe Rabbainu would be drawn. For each of the companions — the students of Moshe — must illuminate in one another. Through this holy <span class="h">da'as</span>, the wondrous illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> is drawn.</div>

<div class="p">This is the wondrous juxtaposition of the verse: <span class="v">"You are beautiful, My beloved, <span class="h">k'Tirtzah</span>"</span> — speaking of the greatness of <span class="h">Ratzon</span>. <span class="v">"Comely as <span class="h">Yerushalayim</span>"</span> — <span class="h">Yerushalayim</span> = <span class="h">yirah shaleim</span> [complete fear] — for the <span class="h">Ratzon</span> and <span class="h">da'as</span> are drawn through <span class="h">yirah</span>. <span class="v">"Awesome as the bannered hosts"</span> — the <span class="h">degalim</span> — for the illumination of <span class="h">da'as</span> (= <span class="h">Ratzon</span>) is drawn through the banners: all Israel's colors illuminating one another. This is the aspect of each person <span class="kc">speaking with his fellow about <span class="h">yiras Shamayim</span> every day</span>, each receiving from the other — through which the <span class="h">da'as</span> is drawn, through which <span class="h">Ratzon</span> shines.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 48 — "<i>Turn Your Eyes Away</i>": The Boundary of Silence</div>

<div class="p">Juxtaposed to this: <span class="v">"Turn your eyes away from Me"</span> <span class="sr">(Song of Songs 6:5)</span>. This is the aspect of <span class="kc">silence</span> — <span class="mx">"a fence for wisdom is silence."</span> For "eyes" alludes to wisdom. The verse warns: turn aside and remove one's eyes of wisdom from gazing — not to look or examine what one has no permission [to examine] — not to enter <span class="h">kushyos</span> and <span class="h">terutzim</span> that are above time — the aspect of silence.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 49 — Never Despair: The Core of the <i>Da'as</i></div>

<div class="p">The essential drawing of the holy <span class="h">da'as</span> is to illuminate in the heart of every member of Israel, however deep in the exile of body, soul, and possessions, that despite everything <span class="kc">he should not despair of mercy</span>. For Hashem is still with him — <span class="v">"the whole earth is full of His glory."</span> And let him not listen to any incitement or rejection — whether from others or from the thoughts in his own heart — trying to push him away from Hashem, as if hope is already lost, G-d forbid. For it is not so: <span class="kc">even now, Hashem is still with you.</span></div>

<div class="p">Therefore, when the nations and the forces of the other side tell Israel: <span class="v">"Return, return, O Shulamis"</span> <span class="sr">(Song of Songs 7:1)</span> — inciting them to return to [the nations], G-d forbid (as Rashi explains) — Israel responds: <span class="v">"What will you see in the Shulamis? As the dance of the camps"</span> — referring to the camps of the <span class="h">degalim</span> (as Rashi explains). Meaning: how can you entice us to distance from Hashem due to the bitterness of exile? We already have the holiness of the dance of the camps — the holiness of the <span class="h">degalim</span> — from which every person speaks with his fellow about <span class="h">yiras Shamayim</span>, illuminating one another. Therefore you can no longer seduce us from the truth, for wondrous <span class="h">da'as</span> has already been illuminated in us, each person illuminating in his fellow — the holiness of the <span class="h">degalim</span> — through which we remain steadfast in the truth of Israel's holiness.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 50 — Reading the <i>Parshah</i> of the <i>Degalim</i> Before <i>Shavuos</i></div>

<div class="p">For even now, when we have neither <span class="h">Mishkan</span> nor <span class="h">degalim</span> nor <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, the holy Torah that remains with us includes everything, as our Sages say. Therefore, by reading in the Torah the <span class="h">Parshah</span> of the <span class="h">Degalim</span> near <span class="h">Shavuos</span>, we draw upon ourselves the awesome wonders of the holiness of the <span class="h">degalim</span> — the inclusiveness of all the colors.</div>

<div class="p">Each person must intend this: to merit drawing upon himself the holiness of the <span class="h">degalim</span>, so he will strive to <span class="kc">speak with his fellow about <span class="h">yiras Shamayim</span> every day</span>, illuminating the holy <span class="h">da'as</span> in the world by illuminating one another. Also, the <span class="h">degalim</span> are the aspect of <span class="h">remazim</span> — for banners serve as signs and signals and hints for many matters regarding gathering and battle tactics. The soldiers understand many <span class="h">remazim</span> through the banners — to gather, each to his camp and his banner. The battle is mainly conducted through the banners. The <span class="h">degalim</span> are the <span class="h">remazim</span> through which the <span class="h">da'as</span> is drawn — so each person remains standing.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 51 — The Tribe of Yissachar and the Counsel of the Wagons</div>

<div class="p">The Midrash greatly praises this counsel of the <span class="h">nessi'im</span> to bring wagons and oxen to carry the <span class="h">Mishkan</span> — until Moshe feared that perhaps the <span class="h">Ruach HaKodesh</span> had departed from him and rested upon the <span class="h">nessi'im</span>, or another prophet had arisen and innovated the <span class="h">halachah</span> — until Hashem told him that "from them" the idea arose.</div>

<div class="p">Who gave them this counsel? Rabbi Simon said: <span class="kc">the tribe of Yissachar</span> told them: <span class="mx">"This <span class="h">Mishkan</span> you are making — it flies in the air! Make wagons for it so it can be carried upon them."</span> This is why Scripture praises Yissachar: <span class="v">"knowing understanding for the times… to know what Israel should do"</span> <span class="sr">(I Chronicles 12:33)</span>.</div>

<div class="p">For Yissachar is the aspect of the true <span class="h">tzadik</span> who illuminates in all the worlds — upper and lower — who can awaken all the dwellers of the dust lying in the very lowest depths, informing them that Hashem is still with them. This is the aspect of Yissachar — who informs everyone: <span class="kc"><span class="h">yeish sachar</span> [there is reward]</span> — <span class="v">"there is reward for your labor, and there is hope for your future"</span> <span class="sr">(Jeremiah 31:16)</span> — as explained well in Hilchos Peter Chamor.</div>

<div class="p">The <span class="h">tzadik</span>, the aspect of Yissachar, illuminates all the <span class="h">halachos</span> of the Torah in Israel as at Sinai — as the Midrash says on <span class="v">"all their brethren were at their word"</span>: everyone agreed to the <span class="h">halachah</span> on their authority, like <span class="h">halachah l'Moshe miSinai</span>. Until he illuminates in children and students an immense and awesome strengthening — that they should strengthen all their days and times, in whatever passes over them, never to fall or distance from Hashem forever, no matter what.</div>

<div class="p">This is: <span class="v">"and from the children of Yissachar"</span> — the children and students of the <span class="h">tzadik</span> called Yissachar — <span class="v">"knowing understanding for the times"</span> — they know <span class="h">binah</span> for all the times passing over them. About this Dovid cried: <span class="v">"My times are in Your hand; deliver me"</span> <span class="sr">(Psalms 31:16)</span>. For every person knows how many times pass over him — in body, soul, and possessions, in livelihood, in raising children — so many different troubles and so many salvations and kindnesses and wonders. The <span class="h">tzadik</span>, the aspect of Yissachar, illuminates in them counsel for every time — what to do to remain standing.</div>

<div class="p">This is: <span class="v">"to know <span class="h">mah</span> Israel should do"</span> — <span class="kc"><span class="h">mah</span> specifically</span> — the aspect of <span class="v">"at the time it shall be said to Yaakov and Yisrael: <span class="h">mah</span> has G-d wrought!"</span> — the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> = <span class="h">mah</span>. <span class="v">"How great is Your goodness that You have stored for those who fear You"</span> <span class="sr">(Psalms 31:20)</span>. For the essential strengthening is through <span class="h">Ratzon</span>: however things are, wherever one is, at least strengthen with a very strong <span class="h">Ratzon</span> for Hashem — yearning and longing to return from wherever one is. For what will remain of him in the end? Especially since the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> have already revealed that the good <span class="h">Ratzon</span> itself is exceedingly precious.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore, strengthen always with a strong <span class="h">Ratzon</span> for Hashem from wherever you are. Through this, you will be aroused to pray constantly, to say much <span class="h">Tehillim</span> and supplications, that Hashem should have mercy and draw upon you the true compassion of the true leader — the aspect of <span class="v">"for the One who has mercy on them shall lead them."</span> Until you merit to exit the <span class="h">ruach shtus</span>. Especially to engage greatly in <span class="h">hisbodedus</span> — personal prayer between you and your Creator — until your heart pours out like water before Hashem, <span class="v">"until He looks down and sees from heaven"</span> and illuminates the holy <span class="h">da'as</span>. For this prayer is the aspect of <span class="h">yirah</span>, the aspect of <span class="h">mah</span> — <span class="v">"<span class="h">Mah</span> does Hashem your G-d ask of you? Only to fear"</span> <span class="sr">(Deuteronomy 10:12)</span> — the aspect of prayer — <span class="v">"a woman who fears Hashem, she shall be praised"</span> — as this verse is brought regarding the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 52 — The Wagons, the Calf, and <i>Emunah</i></div>

<div class="p">Therefore specifically the tribe of Yissachar, who received this holy <span class="h">da'as</span>, gave the counsel to bring <span class="kc">wagons</span> to carry the <span class="h">Mishkan</span> — where the essential inclusiveness of upper and lower resides. This was the essential <span class="h">tikkun</span> for the <span class="kc">sin of the Golden Calf</span> — where Israel blemished <span class="h">emunah</span> and served idols. When Hashem was appeased toward Moshe, He commanded the <span class="h">Mishkan</span> to be built with awesome <span class="h">tikkunim</span> — to draw His <span class="h">Shechinah</span> below and reveal that <span class="v">"the whole earth is full of His glory."</span></div>

<div class="p">Yissachar's profound counsel: bring wagons and oxen to carry the <span class="h">Mishkan</span>, so that through carrying it, they could draw His G-dliness below into all the lowest levels — informing all that <span class="v">"Hashem — He is the G-d."</span> For this, Israel traveled in the wilderness forty years and forty-two journeys, traversing many deserts — all to reveal that <span class="v">Hashem is in every place</span>.</div>

<div class="p">This is hinted in the Midrash where they said: <span class="mx">"This <span class="h">Mishkan</span> that you are making — it flies in the air!"</span> Meaning: it is exceedingly far from this material world and could fly away and depart from you. Therefore make wagons and oxen to carry it — <span class="kc">draw its holiness down even onto the physical wagons and oxen, animals devoid of <span class="h">da'as</span></span>. For the more Israel draws His holiness lower and lower, revealing that His holiness rests even in the depth of physicality, the more His Name is elevated and sanctified. And through this specifically, His holiness is bound below and will not depart — as understood from the Torah teaching, the Holy Zohar, and the Tikkunim (especially: "Israel calls to Her with <span class="h">Shema Yisrael</span> and binds Her with many knots of <span class="h">tzitzis</span> and <span class="h">tefillin</span>…" the aspect of <span class="v">"a King bound in the tresses"</span> brought in <span class="h">Rabbainu</span>'s writings, <span class="lr">LM II, 60</span>).</div>

<div class="p">The wheels of the wagons carrying the <span class="h">Mishkan</span> are the aspect of the <span class="h">Ma'aseh Merkavah</span> [the Chariot] — wheels (<span class="h">ofanim</span>) and <span class="h">galgalim</span> bearing the Throne. These wheels of the wagons — the wheels of the Chariot — are the aspect of the <span class="kc"><span class="h">galgal hachozer</span></span> [turning wheel] brought in <span class="h">Rabbainu</span>'s writings (regarding the <span class="h">dreydl</span>, brought in <span class="h">Likutay Mohara"n</span> near the <span class="h">Sipurai Ma'asiyos</span>) — the aspect of "the upper below and the lower above" — the inclusiveness of the worlds.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 53 — Yaakov and the Wagons of Yosef</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of what is written about Yaakov: <span class="v">"He saw the wagons that Yosef sent to carry him, and the spirit of Yaakov their father was revived"</span> <span class="sr">(Genesis 45:27)</span>. For when the tribes carried Yaakov, they carried him in the aspect of the <span class="h">Mishkan</span> — arranged as Yaakov commanded them to carry him after his passing, as brought in the words of our Sages and Rashi's commentary. Certainly during Yaakov's lifetime all his travels were in this aspect — the travels of the tribes with the <span class="h">Mishkan</span>. The main thing is to merit this during one's lifetime — <span class="mx">"today to do them," "whoever labored on the eve of Shabbos."</span></div>

<div class="p">Yaakov sanctified himself until he merited in his lifetime that all his travels were in this aspect. Yosef knew this secret, for Yaakov transmitted all his wisdom to him. Therefore, when he saw the wagons Yosef sent, <span class="v">"Yaakov's spirit was revived"</span> — he understood that only Yosef knew this secret: the secret of the <span class="h">nessi'im</span>'s wagons carrying the <span class="h">Mishkan</span>, the order of travel with the <span class="h">degalim</span> that all was received from Yaakov their father.</div>

<div class="p">Through this secret, the essential <span class="h">tikkun</span> of <span class="h">emunah</span> is drawn — nullifying the sin of the Calf (the blemish of <span class="h">emunah</span>). Through all the <span class="h">tikkunim</span> of the <span class="h">Mishkan</span> with the wagons, the <span class="h">Shechinah</span> is drawn below in a strong, firm bond that will never be severed — so that <span class="kc">even now, in these end of days, at the end of exile, in the bitterness of the bitterness of the soul's exile of these times</span>, the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> will illuminate in us so that we know how to strengthen at every time — in the aspect of <span class="v">"the children of Yissachar, knowing understanding for the times, to know what Israel should do."</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 54 — Why <i>HaMotzi</i> Is Greater Than All <i>Berachos</i></div>

<div class="p">Let us return to the <span class="kc"><span class="h">halachos</span> of the meal and <span class="h">betzi'as hapas</span></span>. The <span class="h">berachah</span> of <span class="h">HaMotzi</span> is esteemed and takes precedence over all <span class="h">birchos hanehenin</span> [blessings on enjoyments], as explained in the <span class="h">Poskim</span>. For the essential satisfaction and sustaining of the heart is through bread — the primary human food. As our Sages say, brought by Rashi on the verse: <span class="v">"Let me fetch a morsel of bread that you may sustain your heart"</span> <span class="sr">(Genesis 18:5)</span> — in Torah, Prophets, and Writings we find that bread sustains the heart. The essential satisfaction is from the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span>, drawn from the hands in <span class="h">yam hachochmah</span> — the <span class="h">remazim</span> — <span class="v">"You open Your hand…"</span></div>

<div class="p">For the essential life-force a person receives from food is through the wisdom and <span class="h">da'as</span> clothed within the food — <span class="v">"wisdom gives life."</span> The essential completeness of <span class="h">da'as</span> is the <span class="h">remazim</span> the <span class="h">Rav</span> illuminates to his students with his hands. Through this comes the essential strength in <span class="kc">battle</span> — every person must fight in this world the long, great war against the <span class="h">yetzer hara</span>. We have no strength except through the Torah received from our Sages who explained Moshe's Torah and showed us many paths and good counsels. The essential <span class="h">da'as</span> can only illuminate through <span class="h">remazim</span> — the aspect of hands. For it is impossible to tell every person how to withstand everything that will pass over him all his life — only the wise one who desires truth must set his heart to understand the <span class="h">remazim</span>, the hands in <span class="h">yam hachochmah</span>, so he remains firm in his Jewish essence forever.</div>

<div class="p">This is what Dovid HaMelech said: <span class="v">"Blessed is Hashem my Rock, who trains my <span class="kc">hands</span> for battle, my <span class="kc">fingers</span> for war"</span> <span class="sr">(Psalms 144:1)</span>. The essential training for this war is through the hands and ten fingers — the <span class="h">remazim</span>, the aspect of <span class="v">"I stretched out my hands"</span> — included in all ten fingers, with many <span class="h">remazim</span> in each finger — <span class="v">"my fingers for war."</span></div>

<div class="p">Because in <span class="kc">bread</span>, the <span class="h">da'as</span> with the <span class="h">remazim</span> is most clothed — therefore it has the greatest power to satisfy and sustain the heart. The essential satisfaction through the <span class="h">remazim</span> — the hands — through which the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> is drawn. Therefore bread is called <span class="h">lechem</span> — the language of <span class="h">milchamah</span> [war], as it is written: <span class="v">"<span class="h">lachem</span> [consume] my bread"</span> <span class="sr">(Numbers 14:9)</span>. The Holy Zohar says: <span class="mx">"at the time of the meal is the time of battle."</span> One must strive greatly during eating to draw the holy illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> — not be a lazy man. Therefore the Torah is compared to bread: <span class="v">"Come, eat of my bread"</span> <span class="sr">(Proverbs 9:5)</span> — for Torah is the <span class="h">da'as</span> from which bread receives its life-force.</div>

<div class="p">This is the <span class="h">berachah</span> of <span class="h">HaMotzi</span>: it differs from all <span class="h">birchos hanehenin</span> in that it specifies the <span class="kc">source</span> of the bread — <span class="v">"Who brings forth bread <span class="kc">from the earth</span>."</span> We mention "the earth" explicitly — unlike other <span class="h">berachos</span> (<span class="v">"Who creates the fruit of the tree,"</span> <span class="v">"of the ground,"</span> <span class="v">"by Whose word everything came to be"</span>) which do not mention that they came "from the earth" explicitly, even though everything did. Because the essential satisfaction and life-force is through bread most of all, this <span class="h">berachah</span> is esteemed above all, mentioning explicitly: He brought forth the bread from the earth — meaning: He created the bread and invested in it the life-force, the wisdom and <span class="h">da'as</span> clothed within it. And this <span class="h">da'as</span> — the life-force within it — He brought forth <span class="kc">from the earth specifically</span>: from the aspect of <span class="v">"the whole earth is full of His glory."</span> <span class="v">"The whole earth"</span> specifically — for His G-dliness is revealed specifically in this earth. This is the entire Torah teaching: showing those below that <span class="v">"the whole earth is full of His glory"</span> — Hashem has seraphim and angels and exalted worlds, yet He sets them all aside and rests His <span class="h">Shechinah</span> below, specifically in this physical world. From this aspect, Hashem brings forth the bread to give life to man.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore the <span class="h">berachah</span> on bread is esteemed and takes precedence, exempting all items that come on account of the meal. As stated in the <span class="h">Mishnah</span>: <span class="mx">"One who blessed on the bread exempts the side-dishes; one who blessed on the side-dishes does not exempt the bread."</span> For the essential completeness of <span class="h">da'as</span> is most clothed in bread — all the <span class="h">remazim</span> of <span class="h">yam hachochmah</span> are hidden within it. We see with our senses: all other foods have pleasant and sweet tastes more than bread — yet the essential satisfaction and life-force is bread. This is because all tastes derive from <span class="h">da'as</span>, called <span class="h">ta'am</span> [taste/reason], as it is written: <span class="v">"good taste and <span class="h">da'as</span> teach me"</span> <span class="sr">(Psalms 119:66)</span>. Each food receives some portion of <span class="h">da'as</span> and accordingly has a pleasant taste. But in bread, one does not perceive such sweetness — yet in truth, <span class="kc">all the tastes of all the foods in the world are hidden within bread</span>, for there the essential <span class="h">da'as</span> is clothed in its completeness — the <span class="h">remazim</span> of <span class="h">yam hachochmah</span>. Therefore bread is esteemed above all foods, even those with very pleasant tastes — for all their sweetness, derived from the <span class="h">da'as</span> where all true sweetness resides, has no completeness except through bread.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 55 — "<i>One Thing I Have Asked</i>": <i>Ratzon</i> in Battle</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Though a camp encamp against me, my heart shall not fear; though war arise against me, <span class="kc">in this</span> I trust. <span class="kc">One thing</span> I have asked of Hashem, that I seek — to dwell in the house of Hashem all the days of my life, to behold the pleasantness of Hashem"</span> <span class="sr">(Psalms 27:3-4)</span>.</div>

<div class="p">The essential strength in the war of this world is through the <span class="h">remazim</span> — the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span>: "despite everything, I desire the truth." Dovid says: though a camp encamps against me, though war arises — <span class="v">"in this I trust."</span> And he explains what "this" is that he trusts in: <span class="v">"<span class="kc">One thing</span> I have asked of Hashem… to dwell in the house of Hashem all my life, to behold the pleasantness…"</span> This is the strengthening of holy <span class="h">Ratzon</span> and yearnings: all my asking and seeking from Hashem always is only <span class="kc">one thing</span> — to dwell in Hashem's house, to behold His pleasantness — holy <span class="h">Ratzon</span>. Since all my desire is only this, and all my asking and seeking throughout my life is only this — <span class="v">"in this I trust"</span> — in all the wars that arise against me, in all the camps encamping and surrounding me from every side: despite everything, I will not fear, for <span class="kc">I trust in this</span> — the strengthening of holy <span class="h">Ratzon</span> that I strengthen with at all times. Through this, Hashem will help me triumph.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 56 — Ben Zoma's <i>Mishnah</i>: Wise, Mighty, Rich, Honored</div>

<div class="p">This is the juxtaposition of the <span class="h">Mishnah</span> in <span class="h">Avos</span> Chapter 4 — Ben Zoma says:</div>

<div class="p"><span class="kc">"Who is wise?</span> He who learns from every person, as it says: <span class="v">'From all my teachers I gained understanding'</span>" <span class="sr">(Psalms 119:99)</span>. Certainly this does not mean one should suffice with learning from everyone and not learn from a <span class="h">Rav</span> at all — for our Sages warned: <span class="mx">"Make for yourself a <span class="h">Rav</span>,"</span> and said whoever does not serve a <span class="h">talmid chacham</span>, even if he studied Scripture and <span class="h">Mishnah</span>, is an <span class="h">am ha'aretz</span>. Rather: who is the <span class="kc">wise person</span> who has already received wisdom properly from his true <span class="h">Rav</span> until he deserves to be called wise? He who <span class="kc">learns from every person</span>. When one has received his <span class="h">Rav</span>'s wisdom properly by understanding the <span class="h">remazim</span>, then he can learn from every person and perceive in everyone <span class="h">remazim</span> for drawing close to Hashem. As <span class="h">Rabbainu</span> wrote (<span class="lr">LM I, 54</span>): Hashem contracts Himself from infinity to finitude and hints to every person every day <span class="h">remazim</span> to draw close to His service — through every thought, word, and action. But to understand this, one must be very fluent in the Torah received from one's <span class="h">Rav</span> with its <span class="h">remazim</span>. Then one can find the words of his holy Torah in all the conversations of the world — in all conversations, there is hidden Torah. The proof-text: <span class="v">"From all my teachers I gained understanding, <span class="kc">for Your testimonies are my conversation</span>"</span> — in all conversations I find Your testimonies (= Torah). Through this, I gain understanding from all my teachers — learning from every person.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="kc">"Who is mighty?</span> He who conquers his <span class="h">yetzer</span>." For the essential might and victory in war is through the <span class="h">remazim</span> — the hands. The proof-text: <span class="v">"Better is the patient one than the mighty"</span> — the essential might is patience (<span class="h">arichas apayim</span>), extending patience over everything that passes all one's life, never falling from anything — as explained in the Torah teaching on patience (<span class="lr">LM I, 155</span>). All this is received only through the <span class="h">remazim</span> — the wisdom from which one learns from everyone how to save oneself. This is the essential wisdom.</div>

